---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config';
const updated = '2026-11-11';
---

<Base title="Privacy" description="What PQC Deps collects (very little), why, and who processes it.">
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="muted">Last updated {updated}</p>
    <p class="lede">
      {SITE.name} is built to need as little data as possible. There are no accounts for visitors,
      no advertising and no tracking cookies.
    </p>

    <h2>Browsing the site</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The site is hosted on <strong>Cloudflare Pages</strong>. Like any web host, Cloudflare
        processes technical data such as your IP address to deliver pages and protect against
        attacks.
      </li>
      <li>
        We use <strong>Cloudflare Web Analytics</strong> to count page views. It sets no cookies,
        doesn't use your IP address to identify you, and doesn't track you across sites. We see
        only totals such as page views, countries and referring sites.
      </li>
      <li>We set no cookies for visitors.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>"Check my project"</h2>
    <p>
      The dependency checker runs entirely in your browser. The file you paste or upload is never
      sent to us or anyone else. The only request the page makes is to download the public catalog.
    </p>

    <h2>Requests you send us</h2>
    <p>
      If you use <em>Request a re-check</em> or <em>Request a library</em>, what you type is
      published as a <strong>public GitHub issue</strong> in our repository so maintainers can
      review it. Don't include personal information. We don't ask for your name or email.
    </p>
    <p>
      To stop spam, these forms use <strong>Cloudflare Turnstile</strong>, which checks that a human
      is sending the request. Turnstile processes technical signals from your browser for that
      check only.
    </p>

    <h2>Maintainers</h2>
    <p>
      Maintainers sign in with GitHub. We ask GitHub only for the username (no other permissions),
      don't store the GitHub access token, and keep a signed session cookie for up to 12 hours. It
      exists only for maintainers who sign in.
    </p>

    <h2>Links to other sites</h2>
    <p>
      Library pages link to release notes, documentation and GitHub. Those sites have their own
      privacy policies.
    </p>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p>
      Open an issue on <a href={SITE.repoUrl}>GitHub</a>. For anything you'd rather not post
      publicly, see our <a href={`${SITE.repoUrl}/security/policy`}>security policy</a> for a
      private contact route.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
